Route B (selfhost) G15 -- arm the activation hook BEFORE the `!init_result`
early return, so a SECOND FlutterEngine in one process is armed too.

THE SEVERE HALF OF G15, and the filed diagnosis was right about the effect and
wrong about the mechanism. PARITY §14b said "the hook is armed once per process;
engine two never arms it". Nothing is armed once per process. Arming is attempted
on every ConfigureShorebird call -- but it sat BELOW an early return whose
condition is "the updater initialized", and the updater deliberately refuses to
initialize twice:

  third_party/updater/library/src/config.rs, set_config():
      if config.is_some() {
        bail!("Updater already initialized, ignoring second shorebird_init call.");
      }

...a case whose own comment says it "happens regularly with apps that use Firebase
Messaging". So for engine two, shorebird_init returns false BY DESIGN,
RealUpdater::Init returns false, `if (!init_result) return;` fires, and
InstallRouteBActivationHook is never reached. Engine two's root isolate then runs
the ORIGINAL AOT code while engine one runs the patch: two Flutter engines in one
process executing different program versions, with no error, no log and no
user-visible failure. Add-to-app hosts create engines lazily and sometimes more
than once, so this is a mainstream configuration rather than a corner.

THE FIX IS A MOVE, NOT A REDESIGN, and it is safe in both directions:
  * second call          the updater is already initialized from the first, so the
                         patch resolution above ran normally and route_b_path is
                         correct. (route_b_path is declared at function scope, well
                         above both positions.)
  * genuine init failure NextBootPatchPath yields nothing, route_b_path is empty,
                         and InstallRouteBActivationHook returns inert -- which is
                         its documented contract and is now pinned by a test.

The auto-update thread start stays below the guard, where it belongs: starting two
update threads in one process is exactly what that early return is for.

PRECEDENT, directly above the changed line: shorebird_report_launch_start was moved
out of ConfigureShorebird for the same class of reason -- "This fixes issues with
FlutterEngineGroup and other cases where ConfigureShorebird() is called but no
Shell is created". Launch reporting was fixed for multi-engine; the Route B arming
was left behind the guard.

ALSO IN THIS PATCH: InstallRouteBActivationHook is declared in shorebird.h
("Exposed for testing", following GetValueFromYaml's precedent) and three tests
pin the arming contract, where there was previously no coverage at all:
inert on an empty path, armed for a patch path even when the file does not exist
(the container is opened later, from the callback), and an existing callback
CHAINED rather than replaced.

VERIFICATION STATE, stated exactly:
  * shorebird.cc and the test translation unit both COMPILE
    (obj/.../shorebird.shorebird.o and
     obj/.../shorebird_unittests.shorebird_unittests.o, 1/1 each).
  * The tests are NOT RUN. `shorebird_unittests` cannot LINK in the Route B tree
    because it also compiles patch_cache_unittests.cc, whose subject calls
    Shorebird_ReadLinkHeader -- a symbol that exists only in Shorebird's PRIVATE
    Dart fork, which this vanilla-Dart tree does not have. That is a pre-existing
    property of the target, not of this change, and it is the same trap
    build_ios_release.sh documents for shorebird_use_interpreter.
    To run them: build shorebird_unittests in the SHIPPING iOS tree, or add a test
    target that excludes patch_cache_unittests.cc.
  * The ordering itself is therefore verified by source and by compile, NOT by a
    passing test, and the two-engine behaviour is NOT yet verified on a device.
    That gate needs a mint.

Applies on top of the Route B engine series.
